# Introduction

`hive` is a formalization, extension and optimization of what is typically known as *bucket arrays* in game programming circles. Similar data-structures are used in high-performance computing, high performance trading, 3D-simulation, particle simulations, robotics fields. 

```{tikz}
#| fig-cap: "Swiss table memory layout"
#| echo: false
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, fit, backgrounds}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  slot/.style={draw, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm, font=\small\ttfamily},
  ctrl/.style={draw, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm, fill=blue!15, font=\small\ttfamily},
  empty/.style={draw, minimum width=1.2cm, minimum height=0.8cm, fill=gray!20, font=\small\ttfamily},
]

% Control bytes row
\node[ctrl] (c0) at (0,0) {0x3A};
\node[ctrl, right=0 of c0] (c1) {0x80};
\node[ctrl, right=0 of c1] (c2) {0x51};
\node[empty, right=0 of c2] (c3) {0x80};
\node[ctrl, right=0 of c3] (c4) {0x2F};
\node[ctrl, right=0 of c4] (c5) {0x80};
\node[ctrl, right=0 of c5] (c6) {0x7C};
\node[ctrl, right=0 of c6] (c7) {0x19};

% Label above
\node[above=0.3cm of c0, anchor=south] {\small slot 0};
\node[above=0.3cm of c3, anchor=south] {\small empty};

% Slots row below
\node[slot, below=0.6cm of c0] (s0) {key0};
\node[slot, right=0 of s0] (s1) {key1};
\node[slot, right=0 of s1] (s2) {key2};
\node[empty, right=0 of s2] (s3) {---};
\node[slot, right=0 of s3] (s4) {key4};
\node[slot, right=0 of s4] (s5) {---};
\node[slot, right=0 of s5] (s6) {key6};
\node[slot, right=0 of s6] (s7) {key7};

% Labels
\node[left=0.3cm of c0] {\small ctrl};
\node[left=0.3cm of s0] {\small slots};

% Arrows
\draw[->] (c0.south) -- (s0.north);
\draw[->] (c4.south) -- (s4.north);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

